PLACES=[
N('carson-roller-mill','Carson Roller Mill','Carson','nd','industry','standing',46.41833,-101.56389,'site','view','drive','','1913',
  'A three-story wood-frame flour mill built in 1913 by a group of Carson businessmen. By mid-1914 it was making 45 barrels a day of "Wild Rose Flour", and by 1918 it shipped flour as far as Minneapolis and Boston. The Muggli brothers of Glen Ullin bought it in 1919, and Vincent Muggli ran it until 1960. The National Register form calls it the only known roller flour mill in North Dakota left essentially unaltered with its original equipment.',
  'The outside is sheathed in stamped sheet metal. The Register form (1980) says the inside holds the roller mills and grain cleaners installed from 1913 to 1919 and a 32-horsepower Fairbanks Morse kerosene engine in the basement that drives belts to the third floor, with wooden chutes between floors. A 1980 article says it has been kept in working order since 1960 to clean and store grain for the Muggli farms. Whether it is still used, and whether anyone shows it, was not found.',
  'The one North Dakota flour mill that still has its 1913 machinery.',
  'Not found: who owns it today, or any page that gives visiting times. It was a private business in 1980 (the Muggli family). Look at it from the street in Carson; do not go in without asking. The town office, the Grant County courthouse at 106 2nd Avenue NE, may know whom to ask. No parking lot is mapped; park on the street.',
  [W('Carson Roller Mill'),NR('80002915'),['North Dakota History (1980), reprinted in Gas Engine Magazine','https://www.gasenginemagazine.com/gas-engines/carson-roller-mill/']],brick='no',conf='mixed'),
N('lisbon-opera-house','Lisbon Opera House','Lisbon','nd','civic','standing',46.44217,-97.68108,'site','open','drive','','1889',
  'A three-story brick building on Main Street, built in 1889. The ground floor was shop space (later a J.C. Penney store) and the hall was above. It was used as an opera house until about 1910, then for motion pictures until 1922. The Register form calls it one of a few remaining prairie opera houses and one of the largest and finest of its kind.',
  'The 1978 Register form describes a main hall on the second and third floors with brown stenciling on cream walls, a curved proscenium, a horseshoe balcony hung from the roof by steel rods with maroon velvet seats, and a stage with oil footlamps and dressing rooms behind it. Plaster was falling in places then. The ground floor was altered in the 1950s. What the hall looks like today was not checked; the Lisbon Opera House Foundation offers tours.',
  'A prairie opera house with its stenciled hall and balcony, shown by appointment.',
  'The Lisbon Opera House Foundation runs it and says tours are by appointment only (its page is marked 2017, so it may be out of date). The state tourism listing, as a search result quotes it, gives the address as 413 Main Street and the telephone as 701-683-2261, free admission. Park on Main Street. The ground floor is a separate shop space.',
  [W('Lisbon Opera House'),NR('79003727'),['Lisbon Opera House Foundation','https://www.lisbonoperahouse.org/about-us/']],brick='?',hours='Tours by appointment only (foundation page, marked 2017)'),
N('vang-lutheran-church-manfred','Vang Lutheran Church','Manfred','nd','worship','standing',47.69528,-99.75056,'site','open','drive','','1904–1906',
  'A white Norwegian Lutheran church with a 35-foot spire in the village of Manfred, Wells County. The congregation formed in 1894 and held services in sod houses and a schoolhouse. It bought the lot for $175 in 1904, farmers hauled stone for the foundation, a South Dakota contractor built it for $2,155, and the bell came by rail from Minneapolis. The first service was held in September 1906 without pews, which arrived that Christmas. It was dedicated in 1910 with seven pastors present.',
  'A white frame church on a grassy lot, with a spire shingled in 1906. Prairie Public (2019) says the congregation later moved it to a new foundation with a basement lunchroom and changed the lighting from kerosene to electric; the pews date from 1906. It is listed on the National Register, and the Register form says an old two-story brick schoolhouse stands next to it. Interior not checked.',
  'A 1906 prairie church with its spire, still holding services.',
  'Owned by the Vang Evangelical Lutheran congregation (ELCA). Prairie Public (2019) says services are held every other week from Easter to Christmas; times were not found, so ask locally. Manfred is a tiny unincorporated village on a county road; park on the street by the church.',
  [W('Vang Evangelical Lutheran Church'),NR('01000674'),['Prairie Public: Vang Lutheran Church (2019)','https://news.prairiepublic.org/main-street/2019-06-26/vang-lutheran-church']],brick='no',hours='Services every other week, Easter to Christmas; times not found (Prairie Public, 2019)'),
N('ray-opera-house','Ray Opera House','Ray','nd','civic','standing',48.34472,-103.16667,'site','open','drive','','1904',
  'A two-story frame building on Main Street built in 1904 by the merchant Sigbjorn Charlson. His grocery and general store filled the ground floor and the family lived at the back, while the opera house was upstairs. It was listed on the National Register in 1978 and is now a museum of the town\'s history, kept by local volunteers.',
  'The 1978 Register form describes a frame building with a fieldstone foundation and a curved roof hidden behind a false front of stamped metal made to look like rusticated stone blocks. Prairie Public (2024) says it is now a museum. What is on display, and whether the stage and hall are intact, was not found.',
  'A store with a stage upstairs, now the town museum.',
  'Run by volunteers; the page of the town of Epping, which lists nearby attractions, says it is open on summer Sundays 2 to 5 p.m. and on weekdays by appointment (701-568-3578 or 701-568-3378). That page is a third party\'s listing and the hours may have changed. Park on Main Street.',
  [W('Ray Opera House'),NR('78001997'),['Prairie Public: Ray Opera House (2024)','https://news.prairiepublic.org/show/the-great-american-folk-show/2024-07-19/former-opera-house-in-ray-north-dakota-preserves-the-towns-history'],['Epping: nearby attractions','https://epping.govoffice.com/?SEC=A1A61732-1CF1-4946-88CC-76D074404288']],brick='no',hours='Summer: Sunday 2 to 5 p.m.; weekdays by appointment (listing on Epping town site)'),
N('wilton-soo-line-depot','Wilton Soo Line Depot','Wilton','nd','rail','standing',47.15889,-100.78306,'site','open','drive','','1900',
  'A railroad depot built in 1900, a year after Wilton was founded, to a design by the Minneapolis architect William J. Keith for the town\'s founder, William D. Washburn. Its top story carries a low tower shaped like a Japanese pagoda; the city says nobody knows why, but Washburn collected Oriental art. The Soo Line closed the station on December 31, 1970. It gave the building to a Bicentennial committee on condition that it be moved off the railroad ground, and it was moved on May 25, 1976. It is now a railroad museum.',
  'The 1978 Register form describes a clapboarded frame building, with brackets under wide eaves, a pentagonal bay window where the agent watched the tracks, the waiting room with wooden wainscot and a ticket window, and the agent\'s three-room apartment upstairs. It was lengthened from 68 to 92 feet in 1907. The form says the interior had been stripped, so what the museum shows now was not checked.',
  'A depot with a pagoda on its roof, saved and moved by the town.',
  'The City of Wilton\'s page says it is a railroad museum but gives no visiting days or times (the hours on that page are the city office\'s). It stands a block north of Main Street. Park on the street.',
  [W('Wilton station (North Dakota)'),NR('78003079'),['City of Wilton: Soo Line Depot','https://www.wiltonnd.org/wilton-soo-line-depot/']],brick='no',hours='?'),
N('rugby-great-northern-depot','Great Northern Passenger Depot (Rugby station)','Rugby','nd','rail','standing',48.3697,-99.9977,'exact','view','drive','','1907',
  'A one-and-a-half-story Tudor Revival depot built in 1907 for the Great Northern Railway. Local people restored it in 1987, and it was listed on the National Register in 1991. It is still the Amtrak stop for Rugby on the Empire Builder, and the railway, tracks and station belong to BNSF. Amtrak finished a platform renovation in 2025.',
  'The Register form says the walls are faced in brick laid in stretcher bond, and the waiting room has a terrazzo floor with a Greek-key border and a high wainscot of glazed tile. It also lists the wood-framed ticket counter with a brass screen, the conductor\'s counter with frosted glass etched with the Great Northern logo, an original baggage cart and one of the original Rugby signs. The form was written before the 2025 work, so what survives now was not checked.',
  'A 1907 Great Northern depot with its tile waiting room, still used by Amtrak.',
  'An active station owned by BNSF Railway, so keep off the tracks and platform edge. The waiting room is the part visitors use; its opening hours were not found (Amtrak\'s station page was not read). Street parking is mapped on 2nd Avenue Southwest about 60 m away.',
  [W('Rugby station (North Dakota)'),NR('91001466')],brick='?',th=[48.36923,-99.99726],thn='2nd Avenue Southwest parking'),
N('fairview-lift-bridge-cartwright','Fairview Lift Bridge and Cartwright Tunnel','Cartwright','nd','rail','standing',47.85872,-103.96639,'site','view','walk','','1913',
  'A vertical-lift railroad bridge across the Yellowstone River, built in 1913 by Gerrick & Gerrick of Steele for the Montana Eastern Railway, with a tunnel just east of it. Its near twin crosses the Missouri River at Snowden, Montana. A local article says it was built for both trains and cars and that the lift span never raised for a boat; a highway truss bridge of 1955 now stands a few hundred feet north. The railway has not been used since the mid-1980s. A local rumor says a lynched railroad worker lies in an abutment.',
  'Steel towers rise about 108 feet over the river. Wikipedia and a local article describe a timber-lined railroad tunnel about 300 feet east of the bridge, the only completed one in the state, and a fenced walkway with benches on the bridge. Sources disagree about whether the bridge and tunnel are open as a path, and a 2019 article says the east end of the tunnel is collapsing. Their present state was not checked.',
  'North Dakota\'s only lift bridge, with its only railroad tunnel beside it.',
  'The 1996 Register listing names BNSF Railway as owner; one local guide treats the bridge as a walking path and another says the tunnel\'s east end is collapsing. Treat the bridge and tunnel as view-only until the owner or the county says otherwise; do not enter the tunnel. The 2019 article says a parking area is reached through Sundheim Park beside the river; no lot was checked on the aerial.',
  [W('Fairview Lift Bridge'),['Beautiful Badlands ND: the lift bridge and tunnel (2019)','https://www.beautifulbadlandsnd.com/the-only-liftbridge-and-tunnel/']],brick='no',conf='mixed'),
N('ellendale-opera-house-block','Ellendale Opera House Block','Ellendale','nd','civic','standing',46.00167,-98.52833,'site','view','drive','','1908',
  'A three-story building on Main Street, built in 1908 and opened in 1909 to a design by George Issenhuth, with Chicago School touches. A theater with a seating floor was on the main level and offices were on the second story. Fire has taken much of Ellendale\'s older architecture, which is part of why this building was listed on the National Register in 1992.',
  'The 1992 Register form gives sandstone foundation, brick walls and terra cotta details, and says the ground floor held a specialty shop and meeting hall while the upper two stories were vacant. Whether the theater inside survives, and the building\'s use today, were not checked.',
  'A Main Street opera house block that outlasted the fires.',
  'Private commercial building on Main Street as far as the Register form shows; owner and hours not found. Look at it from the street. Park on Main Street.',
  [W('Ellendale Opera House Block'),NR('92000354')],brick='?',conf='mixed'),
N('new-hradec-sts-peter-paul-church','Saints Peter and Paul Church','New Hradec','nd','worship','standing',47.00056,-102.88361,'exact','view','drive','','1917',
  'A brick church built in 1917 for a Czech congregation: the only documented Bohemian settlers from the Crimea in America, who founded New Hradec in 1887. Their first wooden church burned in May 1917 and this one was dedicated that August. It was designed by Nick Ressler of Mandan and built by Charles Bakke of Dickinson. From 1918 to 1950 it also housed a school taught by the Sisters of Notre Dame, and the parish used Czech in worship.',
  'The 1986 Register form calls it essentially unaltered: buff brick over a darker brown brick base, Tudor-arched windows with brown brick hoods, an octagonal wood spire over the bell tower, and inside the original oak pews, hardwood floors, brass fixtures and fir woodwork of the choir loft. The form says a cry room was added in 1962. Interior as it is today not checked.',
  'A Czech church built by Crimean settlers, with its oak pews.',
  'A Catholic parish church owned by the parish. No Mass time could be confirmed: two listings give different times and both look old, so telephone the parish before coming. Park at the church.',
  [W('Saints Peter and Paul Church (New Hradec, North Dakota)'),NR('86000161')],brick='no',conf='mixed'),
N('billings-county-courthouse-museum-medora','Billings County Courthouse Museum','Medora','nd','civic','standing',46.91278,-103.52222,'site','open','drive','','about 1880s; 1913',
  'The Billings County courthouse in Medora, whose core is thought to date from the 1880s and which was remodeled in 1913 by the architect John Tester. It was listed on the National Register in 1977 as a link to the first years of settlement in one of the state\'s least populated counties. Since 1986 the Billings County Historical Society has run it as a museum.',
  'Wikipedia lists barbed wire, farm and automotive tools, guns, military items, western riding gear and ethnic displays from local pioneers, a restored courtroom and jail, and a bunkhouse, general store and kitchen. The town\'s own page also lists a barbed-wire collection and says the building is a visitor information center.',
  'A frontier courthouse with a restored courtroom and jail.',
  'Run by the Billings County Historical Society. The Medora page gives Saturday and Sunday, 9 a.m. to 5 p.m., May to September, with tickets from $1 sold at the courthouse. Other listings disagree, so check before a long drive. Park on the street in Medora.',
  [W('Billings County Courthouse'),['Medora: Courthouse Museum','https://medora.com/courthousemuseum/']],brick='?',hours='May to September: Saturday and Sunday 9 a.m. to 5 p.m. (Medora page)'),
N('old-main-bottineau','Old Main, Dakota College at Bottineau','Bottineau','nd','civic','standing',48.83241,-100.44017,'site','view','drive','','1907–1908',
  'The first building of the North Dakota School of Forestry, a two-year college founded at Bottineau in 1894. The legislature paid $25,000 in 1907 and the architect Joseph Shannon designed this Romanesque Revival building, finished in 1908. The college closed it in 2006 for deferred maintenance, and after fund-raising and work that began in 2023 it reopened in October 2024 with nursing and health classrooms.',
  'A Romanesque Revival school building on the campus, listed on the National Register. What original fabric survived the 2023 to 2024 work was not checked.',
  'The college\'s oldest building, shut for 18 years and brought back.',
  'Owned by Dakota College at Bottineau, a state college; it is now a working classroom building, so look at it from the campus grounds. The college\'s page was not read, so there are no visiting hours. Park on campus.',
  [W('Old Main (Dakota College at Bottineau)')],brick='?'),
]
